## [0.2.0] — Brand-accurate palette, copy pass, hero rebuild

### 1. Palette re-derived from sonic.com screenshots

The original brief specified `#00A4E4`. That is **not** the blue Sonic ships.
Rather than eyeball it, the supplied screenshots were decoded with Pillow and
sampled per UI element (dominant colour of a cropped region, plus a saturation
filtered histogram of the whole frame). Results, now the site's tokens:

| Token | Hex | Sampled from |
|---|---|---|
| `--color-sonic` | `#26ABE2` | "Built Different" headline, logo period, arc motif |
| `--color-brand` | `#2378C6` | navbar CHECK AVAILABILITY button |
| `--color-indigo` | `#2B3374` | hero availability-form button |
| `--color-navy` | `#1B214A` | comparison band, "There's No Competition" |
| `--color-navy-deep` | `#10132B` | deepest stop of the same band |
| `--color-band-from/to` | `#295B98` → `#287FB9` | the "Top rated" gradient band |

Contrast was checked before assigning roles: `#2378C6` on white is 4.59:1, so it
carries eyebrows, links and body-weight text. `#26ABE2` on white is only 2.62:1,
so cyan is restricted to dark surfaces (5.87:1 on `#1B214A`), icons and rules —
never body text on white.

Applied colours were then verified in the live DOM via `getComputedStyle`, not
by eye: disclosure bar / hero / footer `#10132B`, how-it-works + table header
`#1B214A`, header CTA `#2378C6`, ZIP submit `#2B3374`, hero accent word and
eyebrow rules `#26ABE2`, CTA band `#2B3374 → #295B98 → #287FB9`.

### 2. Hero rebuilt

The previous hero was a generic dark-panel-plus-card template. Replaced with a
composition drawn from Sonic's own:
- Layered navy gradient stage (`#10132B → #1B214A → #262D5C`) with a cyan bloom.
- **`<ArcMotif />`** — Sonic's signature concentric arcs sweeping in from the
  top-right. 19 static `<circle>` strokes with computed radii and opacities;
  rendered once at build time. No canvas, no WebGL, no animation, nothing on the
  client.
- Legibility scrim over the arcs: a heavy vertical wash below `lg` (where copy
  spans full width) and a left-weighted horizontal wash at `lg`+, mirroring the
  dark overlay Sonic puts on the left of its hero photo. Added after the first
  mobile render showed arcs cutting through the subline.
- Headline carries a cyan accent phrase, as "Built Different" does, and uses
  `text-wrap: balance` to stop a ragged four-line break at desktop width.
- Featured-plan card restyled as glass with a cyan hairline along its top edge.
- **Docked white availability bar** across the foot of the hero, echoing the
  white address form Sonic docks over its hero. It holds the ZIP checker *and*
  the call CTA side by side, so the hero still carries both entry points.

### 3. Copy pass — formal register

| Location | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| H1 | "Lightning-fast fiber internet, with pricing you can read." | "Fiber internet built for speed, priced with complete transparency." |
| Fine print | "Every cost, in one place" | "Complete pricing and fee disclosure" |
| Fine print sub | "…printed in full rather than buried in a footnote." | "…documented in full — every figure available to review before you place an order." |
| How it works | "Three steps from here to installed" | "Three steps to installation" |
| Why Sonic | "Built on its own fiber, and priced that way" | "A network built and operated end to end" |
| Why Sonic sub | "Owning the network end to end is what makes … possible at the same time." | "Owning the infrastructure end to end is what allows Sonic to deliver symmetrical speeds, unlimited data and predictable monthly pricing at the same time." |

Also caught in the same pass, unprompted but in the same casual register:
"Fiber the whole way" → "End-to-end fiber infrastructure"; "Recognized for
service" → "Independently recognized service"; "Unlimited data, every plan" →
"Unlimited data on every plan"; **"Pricing you can read" → "Transparent,
predictable pricing"** (this was the exact phrase rejected in the H1, still
sitting in a Why-Sonic card); "Pick your plan" → "Select your plan"; "Get
installed free" → "Professional installation"; "Questions worth asking first" →
"Frequently asked questions". All step and feature bodies were reworded to the
same register.

### 4. Structural additions
- New `components/SectionHeading.tsx` — one shared header (cyan rule + eyebrow +
  title + optional description, light/dark tones, left/centre align) now used by
  every section, so typographic scale and rhythm cannot drift between them.
- Buttons adopt Sonic's uppercase, letter-spaced CTA treatment. The underlying
  text is still exactly "Call to order" / "Call for pricing"; the casing is a CSS
  `text-transform`, matching Sonic's own "CHECK AVAILABILITY" buttons.
- Wordmark reworked to Sonic's own form: heavy uppercase "SONIC" with a cyan
  period, retailer qualifier beneath.
- Section rhythm for contrast: navy hero → white fiber → tinted bundle → white
  phone → white fine print → **navy how-it-works** → white why-sonic →
  **gradient CTA band** → tinted FAQ → navy footer.

### 5. QA after redesign
- `tsc --noEmit`, `eslint`, `next build` all clean; still fully static.
- Zero horizontal overflow at 320, 375, 768, 1024, 1600px
  (`scrollWidth === clientWidth` at every width).
- 10 `tel:` links, 10 carrying `data-call-cta`, all on the single
  `SITE.phoneHref`. (Down from 11: the hero's inline phone link was absorbed
  into the docked availability bar.)
- Forbidden-content scan still returns zero hits; section order unchanged at
  `hero → fiber → bundle → phone → fine-print → how-it-works → why-sonic → faq`.
- No hardcoded prices in any `.tsx` — `lib/content.ts` remains the only source.

