## [0.3.0] — Specification-led hero copy and a data-plate featured panel

### Headline and subline rewritten (third pass)

Two earlier attempts were rejected as insufficiently professional:
1. "Lightning-fast fiber internet, with pricing you can read."
2. "Fiber internet built for speed, priced with complete transparency."

Both used the same marketing device — a clever parallel construction. The third
pass drops the device entirely for a specification-led statement, the register a
network operator actually writes in:

> **Symmetrical fiber internet up to 10 Gigabits.**
>
> Sonic designs, builds and operates its own fiber-to-the-home network. Upload
> capacity matches download on every tier, data is uncapped, and professional
> installation is included at no charge — with no annual agreement.

The speed figure is **derived**, not typed: `formatSpeedLong(leadPlan().speedDown)`
renders "10 Gigabits", so the headline tracks `lib/content.ts` like everything
else. New helper `formatSpeedLong()` gives the long prose form ("10 Gigabits")
alongside the existing compact `formatSpeed()` ("10 Gig").

Headline is set as two deliberate `block` spans. The second is `whitespace-nowrap`
so "up to" can never split across the break; the first carries `text-balance`,
which is inert once the line fits and only evens out the wrap on narrow screens.
Sizing was chosen by measurement, not estimate: at 1440px the copy column is
593px and the first line needs 640px at 50px type, so the `lg` size is 2.75rem
and the grid was widened from `1.08fr_0.92fr` to `1.15fr_0.85fr`.

### Featured plan panel rebuilt as a data plate

The previous hero card was a generic pricing card — eyebrow, name, price, four
feature bullets, button. Replaced by `components/HeroPlanPlate.tsx`, structured
as a technical specification plate:

- Ruled header (plan name + speed badge), then the dominant rate, then a
  **specification table** — Download / Upload / Data / Agreement / Installation —
  with hairline dividers and tabular numerals.
- Cyan rail down the leading edge; translucent navy pane at `backdrop-blur-xl`.
  **This surface is deliberately photo-ready** — it holds contrast over an image,
  which matters because a background photograph is planned for the hero.
- Rows come from `planSpecRows(plan)`, so the plate is data-driven like the rest.

`Hero.tsx` now carries a marked **BACKGROUND IMAGE SLOT** comment naming exactly
where the artwork layer goes; the gradient stage, both scrims and the plate blur
are already tuned to sit over a photograph, so nothing else needs changing.

### Supporting data-model work
- Spec values shortened so they read correctly beneath a label:
  `dataPolicy` "Unlimited, uncapped data" → "Unlimited, uncapped";
  `contractTerm` "No contract required" → "None required";
  `installation` "Free installation" → "Free".
- Helpers split: `planSpeedRows()` (throughput), `planTermRows()` (commercial
  terms), `planSpecRows()` (both). The plan-card footer now uses `planTermRows()`
  as labelled micro-rows instead of a dot-joined sentence, which the shortened
  values would have made unreadable ("Unlimited, uncapped · None required · Free").
- SEO title and description moved into `SEO` in `lib/content.ts`, with the speed
  derived — the last hardcoded spec figure in any `.tsx` is now gone.
- `viewport.themeColor` corrected from the brief's `#00A4E4` to the sampled
  `#26ABE2`.

### Gotcha worth remembering: Turbopack CSS cache
A newly introduced arbitrary utility (`lg:grid-cols-[1.15fr_0.85fr]`) silently
failed to apply on the dev server — `display:grid` and `gap` from the same class
list worked, but the column template did not, collapsing the hero to one column.
The production build contained the correct rule
(`.lg\:grid-cols-\[1\.15fr_0\.85fr\]{grid-template-columns:1.15fr .85fr}` inside
`@media (min-width:64rem)`), and serving the production build rendered two
columns at 593px/439px. The cause was a stale Turbopack cache that survived a dev
server restart. **Fix: delete `.next` and restart** — restarting alone is not
enough. When a brand-new arbitrary utility appears not to work, verify against a
production build before editing the markup.

### QA
- `tsc --noEmit`, `eslint`, `next build` clean; still fully static.
- Desktop 1440: grid resolves to `593.391px 438.594px`, headline two lines.
- Mobile 320: no horizontal overflow (`scrollWidth === clientWidth === 320`).
- 10 `tel:` links, 10 carrying `data-call-cta`.

