## [0.4.0] — Photography

Five generated photographs supplied and integrated.

### Delivered assets were reworked before use

Files arrived in `public/` root at **1408×768 each, ~8 MB per file — 38.8 MB
total**, which would have undercut the performance goal the brief opens with.
Re-encoded to progressive JPEG at quality 82 with metadata stripped and moved to
`public/images/`:

| Asset | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| hero-living-room | 7866 KB | 130 KB |
| install-technician | 8052 KB | 210 KB |
| fusion-kitchen | 7983 KB | 185 KB |
| home-phone-console | 7873 KB | 134 KB |
| household-devices | 7998 KB | 183 KB |
| **Total** | **38.8 MB** | **843 KB (2.1%)** |

Visually identical at this resolution. Originals preserved in the session
scratchpad under `original-images/` in case a re-export is ever needed.

Note: sources are 1408px wide, so a full-bleed hero on a 2560px display upscales
slightly. Acceptable; regenerate wider if it ever matters.

### Declared in lib/content.ts, not in components
- New `ImageAsset { src, alt, position? }`.
- `ServiceLineMeta.image?` — a service line that declares a photo renders
  card-beside-photo. This is what finally fills the Fusion and Home Phone rows,
  which previously left a single card stranded in white space.
- `MEDIA` record for the hero, installation and household photographs.
- No `.tsx` file holds an image path or alt string.

### Placements
- **Hero** — `hero-living-room.jpg` as `fill` + `priority` (it is the LCP
  element). Layer order: photo → navy alpha tint → cyan bloom → arc motif →
  legibility scrim.
- **Sonic Fusion / Sonic Home Phone** — photo beside the plan card, row centred.
- **Three steps to installation** — restructured from three cards across to
  photo-left / steps-stacked-right, with a navy wash over the image so a bright
  daylight photograph does not punch a hole in the dark section.
- **Why Sonic** — `household-devices.jpg` as a 21:9 editorial band under the
  heading, above the four cards.

### Two problems found and fixed during integration

**1. Navy gradient was set to `mix-blend-multiply`.** The hero photograph is
already dark (mean luminance 57/255) and multiplying near-black navy over it
crushed the shadows flat. Replaced with an alpha ramp — heaviest top-left behind
the headline, lightest bottom-right so the lamp and window read through the
plate.

**2. Side photos were stretched to the plan card's height.** With `h-full`
against a ~713px card, a 1.83:1 source became a **0.79 portrait box** and
`object-cover` discarded **57% of the frame** — the kitchen shot cropped to a
tight face. Fixed by giving every inset photo a fixed `aspect-[4/3]` and
centring the row instead. Measured retention afterwards:

| Frame | Ratio | Kept |
|---|---|---|
| Hero (fill) | 1.94 | 95% of height |
| Fusion / Home Phone | 1.33 | 73% of width |
| Installation | 1.33 | 73% of width |
| Why Sonic band | 2.33 | 79% of height |

Per-image `objectPosition` values were retuned for the 4:3 frame.

### QA
- `tsc`, `eslint`, `next build` clean; still fully static.
- All five assets serve HTTP 200; the optimizer negotiates WebP
  (hero 133 KB JPEG → **60 KB WebP** at w=1920).
- No horizontal overflow at 320px or 1440px.
- 9 `tel:` links, all 9 carrying `data-call-cta`.
- Hero background is `alt=""` + `aria-hidden` (decorative — the headline carries
  the message); the four content photographs have descriptive alt text.

