## [0.5.0] — Hero rebuilt against the reference sites

Two complaints: the background photograph was invisible under the overlay, and
the featured-plan panel still read as a generic template card.

### What the references actually do

Opened both sites rather than guessing.

**kineticfiber.us** — contained rounded hero card; copy left; headline with a
coloured accent phrase; the price as an *inline* lockup in the copy ("fiber
plans start at $19.99 /mo with AutoPay"); one pill call button; the photograph
framed as a panel *inside* the card, not used as a background.

**bestfiberinternet.us** — full-bleed dark hero; headline with a cyan accent on
the second line; ZIP panel with "or call" fine print; and on the right, **three
small stacked benefit chips** (icon + bold label + one-line detail) instead of a
pricing card; a trust strip with checkmarks across the bottom.

Neither puts a bordered pricing card in the hero. That was the problem.

### Changes
- **Overlay cut hard.** Was two stacked layers — a 135° tint peaking at 0.94
  alpha plus a 95° scrim peaking at 0.90, compounding to ~0.99 on the left and
  burying the photograph. Replaced with a *single* 100° wash running
  0.90 → 0.72 → 0.40 → 0.26. The left stays dense enough to hold the headline;
  the right drops to a quarter so the room, lamp and window read. Cyan bloom
  reduced 0.30 → 0.18 and arc opacity 0.52 → 0.34 so neither fogs the image.
- **`HeroPlanPlate.tsx` deleted.** The price now sits inline under a
  "Fiber plans start at" label with the promotional qualifier beside it, and the
  call button sits on the same row — the kineticfiber arrangement.
- **Three glass highlight chips** replace the plate, per bestfiberinternet.
  Translucent (`bg-white/[0.09]`, `backdrop-blur-md`) specifically so the
  photograph stays visible through them. Content comes from a new
  `heroHighlights()` helper derived from the lead plan, so the speed figure
  cannot drift from `PLANS`.
- **Trust chips moved** from inline pills mid-column to a bordered strip across
  the foot of the hero, matching both references.

### Compliance unchanged
The hero still carries both required entry points — verified in the DOM: one
`tel:` link with `data-call-cta` and one `input[autocomplete="postal-code"]`
inside `#hero`. The call button moved from the deleted plate into the copy
column; it was not dropped.

### QA
- `tsc`, `eslint`, `next build` clean; still fully static.
- No horizontal overflow at 375px or 1440px.
- 9 `tel:` links site-wide, all 9 carrying `data-call-cta`.

**Design note for future edits:** the hero's right-hand elements are translucent
on purpose. If anything there becomes an opaque panel, the photograph is buried
again and this whole change is undone.

---

## [0.6.0] — Hero CTA removed; photography as untinted section backgrounds

### Hero "Call to order" button removed — with a caveat

Requested. **This collided with the standing rule that the hero must contain
both a ZIP checker and a call button**, because `HeroPlanPlate` (which held the
other hero call CTA) was deleted in [0.5.0] — so removing this one would have
left the hero with no call path at all.

Resolved the way bestfiberinternet.us does it: the button is gone, and the call
path survives as fine print under the ZIP field —
"Free to check · No obligation · Or call (888) 555-0123" — with the number as a
`tel:` link carrying `data-call-cta`. No second large button, rule intact.
Verified in the DOM: `#hero` contains one `data-call-cta` link (the number
only, no "Call to order" text) and one `input[autocomplete="postal-code"]`.

If the intent was to remove the call path entirely, drop that line from
`ZipChecker.tsx` — but it breaks the hero rule, so it should be a deliberate call.

### Photography as section backgrounds, no colour overlay

Contrast was measured per image before choosing a treatment, because "no
overlay" and "readable text" pull against each other:

| Image | Section | Dark type | White type |
|---|---|---|---|
| `household-devices` | Why Sonic | 2.4:1 ✗ | **7.4:1 ✓** |
| `fusion-kitchen` | Fusion | **5.8:1 ✓** | 3.1:1 ✗ |
| `home-phone-console` | Home Phone | 4.3:1 ⚠ | 3.8:1 ✗ |

- **Fusion / Home Phone** — photograph full-bleed and untinted. Heading and plan
  card sit together in a solid white panel to one side, alternating sides down
  the page. Neither type colour is safe directly on these interiors, so the
  content gets its own surface instead of the image getting a tint.
- **Why Sonic** — photograph full-bleed and untinted. It is dark enough (mean 63,
  86 behind the heading) that white type clears 7.4:1 directly on it; the four
  feature cards stay solid white. The redundant 21:9 band image was removed.
- **Zero gradient overlays** on all three sections, confirmed by walking the
  rendered DOM for `aria-hidden` gradient layers: `bundle` 0, `phone` 0,
  `why-sonic` 0.

### Two problems found during the work

**1. Region mean luminance is not enough.** The Why Sonic heading measured a
safe 7.4:1 on the top-left quadrant but visibly washed out where it crossed the
lamp — an average hides local hotspots. Because `object-cover` on a container
wider than the source ratio ignores the X axis, the lamp could not be moved out
from behind the heading via `objectPosition`. Fixed with a new `.on-photo`
utility that shadows the glyphs rather than tinting the image, so the photograph
is still exactly as supplied.

**2. Full-bleed backgrounds fail on phones.** A 1.83:1 source covering a ~900px
tall phone section crops to a near-useless vertical sliver. Below `lg` the
photograph now renders as a framed 16:10 banner instead, and the section falls
back to a solid colour — `bg-mist` for Fusion/Home Phone, `bg-navy` for Why
Sonic so its white type stays legible. Confirmed: at 375px `#why-sonic`
computes to `rgb(27,33,74)`.

### QA
- `tsc`, `eslint`, `next build` clean; still fully static.
- No horizontal overflow at 375px or 1440px.
- 9 `tel:` links, all 9 carrying `data-call-cta`.
- `react-hooks/immutability` lint error caught and fixed along the way — the
  panel-side alternation was mutating a counter during render; now derived from
  a pre-computed list of photo-bearing line ids.

