## [0.7.0] — Per-section photo treatments

New `ServiceLineMeta.imagePlacement` (`'card' | 'background'`) decides how a
line's photograph is used, so the two lines no longer share one treatment.

### Internet + Home Phone — photograph moved onto the pricing card
- Section reverts to a plain light (`bg-mist`) band; no section background, no
  section overlay.
- `PlanCard` gained an optional `photo` prop. With it the card flips to a dark
  treatment: photograph fills the card, an internal scrim sits over it, and the
  name, features, terms and price lockup all switch to their dark-tone colours.
  The scrim is not optional — plan detail is dense small type and the kitchen
  interior only measures 3.1:1 for white type untreated.
- Verified: `#bundle` has no background image and 0 section overlays; its
  `<article>` contains an `<img>` and computes to `rgb(16,19,43)`.

### Home Phone — tinted background, content centred
- Photograph stays full-bleed, now under a light vertical wash
  (0.60 → 0.74 navy). The mid-tone interior measures 3.8:1 for white type bare;
  the wash lifts it to roughly 7:1.
- Heading switched to `tone="dark"` and `align="center"`; the plan card is
  centred at `max-w-xl`.
- Verified: `#phone` has a background image, exactly 1 overlay, centred heading
  (`text-align: center`, white) and a card whose midpoint sits on the section
  centre line.

### Why Sonic — graded wash instead of shadowed glyphs
The `.on-photo` text-shadow from [0.6.0] fixed the contrast number but read as
a patch rather than a design. Replaced with a proper 115° navy gradient
(0.88 → 0.76 → 0.60) across the photograph: it gives the heading a consistent
field, neutralises the lamp hotspot that the region average had hidden, and
still lets the room show on the right. The `.on-photo` utility was deleted from
`globals.css` rather than left as dead CSS.

### QA
- `tsc`, `eslint`, `next build` clean; still fully static.
- No horizontal overflow at 1440px.
- 9 `tel:` links, all 9 carrying `data-call-cta`.

**Not visually confirmed.** The Browser pane stopped painting for the last part
of this session (screenshot timeouts — the app window was behind another
window), so these three sections were verified by measuring the rendered DOM,
not by eye. Worth a look before shipping.

