## [1.0.0] — Mobile responsiveness pass, GitHub, Vercel readiness

### Mobile audit and fixes

Audited by walking the rendered DOM at 320 / 390 / 414 / 768px rather than by
eye. Two real classes of problem, neither visible from a desktop view:

**Type too small.** 28 elements rendered below 12px — uppercase eyebrow labels
at 11px and the wordmark qualifier at 9px. Applied a mobile-first type floor
across all eight affected components: every small size now carries a phone
value with the refined desktop size restored from `sm:` upward
(`text-[0.75rem] sm:text-[0.6875rem]` and so on). Down to 4 elements below 12px,
both decorative uppercase badges at 10–11px.

**Tap targets too small.** 18 interactive elements under 40px tall, worst of
them the ZIP submit button at 38px and — more seriously — the hero's only call
path, a text link just **16px tall**. Field and submit now sit at ~46px under
`sm`; the phone link is 32px; footer link rows went from 19px to 31px. All clear
the WCAG 2.2 AA 24px minimum, and the primary actions clear 44px.

**Animation on touch.** Lenis now passes `syncTouch: false` explicitly. Touch
scrolling stays native, which preserves momentum, rubber-band and the
browser-chrome hide/show phone users expect; anchor links still ease because
those route through `lenis.scrollTo()`. Entrance reveals were already gated
behind `@media (scripting: enabled)` and disabled under `prefers-reduced-motion`.

**Touch polish.** `-webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent` on interactive
elements, and a `max-width: 100%` floor on `img`/`svg`/`video`.

Result: **zero horizontal overflow at 320, 390, 414 and 768px.**

### Repository

- `.gitattributes` added — `* text=auto eol=lf` plus binary markers for images.
  Authoring is on Windows; Vercel builds on Linux, so line endings are pinned
  rather than left to platform default.
- `/.claude/` added to `.gitignore`; local tooling config is not project code.
- `package.json`: name set to `sonic-authorized-retailer`, `engines.node >=20.9.0`.
- README rewritten: local setup, Vercel steps, how to edit `lib/content.ts`,
  the compliance rules baked into the build, and the placeholder-phone warning.
- Committed and pushed to `github.com/PavithraEbbali/sonic` (`main`, 42 files).
  Verified local HEAD matches `origin/main`.

### Vercel readiness
- `next.config.ts` left at defaults — no `output: 'export'`, so `next/image`
  optimization stays available on Vercel.
- No environment variables required.
- Both routes prerender as static content.
- Photography ships as optimized JPEG (843 KB total) and is re-encoded again to
  AVIF/WebP per breakpoint by the platform image optimizer.

**Note:** the GitHub repository is **public**. Fine for this code — it holds no
secrets — but worth a deliberate decision, since it includes the full pricing
audit and compliance notes in this file.

---

## [1.1.0] — Eight policy pages

### Reference check first
Both reference sites were opened rather than assumed. bestfiberinternet.us runs
five policy documents (`/privacy-policy/`, `/terms-and-conditions/`,
`/disclaimer/`, `/tcpa-consent/`, `/cookie-policy/`); kineticfiber.us runs only
`/privacy-policy`. Their privacy and TCPA pages were read for structure — H1,
then numbered H2 sections, an effective date at the head, contact block at the
foot — which is the shape adopted here. **No text was copied**; all eight
documents are written for this retailer.

### Architecture
- `lib/legal.ts` holds the documents as data (`LEGAL_PAGES`), matching the
  content-as-data pattern already used for plans.
- `app/(policies)/[slug]/page.tsx` maps over them. The `(policies)` route group
  keeps URLs at the root — `/privacy-policy`, not `/legal/privacy-policy`.
- `dynamicParams = false` so a slug outside the list 404s instead of rendering
  on demand; without it, a root-level `[slug]` would swallow every missing URL.
  Confirmed: `/not-a-real-page` returns 404, `/` still returns 200.
- `components/LegalDocument.tsx` renders one document: navy masthead, then a
  measure-limited column. Supports `**bold**` and nothing else.
- Footer policy list is generated from `LEGAL_PAGES`, so adding a ninth document
  lists it automatically.

### The eight
`privacy-policy` (Privacy & Data Protection) · `disclaimer` · `cookies-policy` ·
`tcpa-policy` · `trademarks` · `marketing-policy` · `service-fulfillment` ·
`pci-dss`. All eight prerender as static HTML — 12 routes total.

Two are worth noting because they are written against how this build actually
works, not from a template:
- **PCI DSS** states plainly that no part of the site collects, processes or
  stores card data — there is no checkout — and lists what representatives will
  never ask for. That is accurate: the site has no payment path at all.
- **Service Fulfillment** separates our role (verify address, explain rates,
  submit order) from the operator's (provision, install, bill), without routing
  anyone to carrier support, which the standing rules forbid.

### Navigation fix this forced
Every in-page anchor was bare (`#plans`), which does nothing from a policy page.
All are now root-relative (`/#plans`), and `SmoothScroll` compares
`anchor.pathname` against `window.location.pathname` so it only eases the scroll
when the link targets the current page — otherwise the browser navigates and
lands on the hash. Internal links converted to `next/link` (ESLint
`no-html-link-for-pages` caught two `<a>` elements); `tel:` links stay as `<a>`.

### Placeholders — must be replaced before launch
`LEGAL` in `lib/legal.ts`: `entityName`, `address`, `email`, `siteName`,
`effectiveDate`. They render visibly as `[Retailer legal entity name]` etc. so
they cannot ship unnoticed. A policy needs an effective date to be enforceable,
which is why one exists here despite the site's no-date-stamp rule — that rule
targets pricing claims.

**These documents have not been reviewed by a lawyer.** They are conventional,
carefully written and matched to this build, but they are not legal advice.

### QA
- `tsc`, `eslint`, `next build` clean. 12 routes, all static.
- All 8 policy URLs return 200 with correct titles; unknown slugs 404.
- No horizontal overflow on a policy page at 320px; 11 sections render.
- Footer lists all 8; `tel:` links still carry `data-call-cta`.

---

## [1.1.1] — Year removed from the footer copyright

`© 2026 Independent Authorized Retailer of Sonic.` → `© Independent Authorized
Retailer of Sonic.`

Dropping it also removed the last `new Date()` call in the tree, so the footer
is now fully deterministic — a statically prerendered page no longer bakes in
the year it happened to be built, and cannot go stale on 1 January.

Confirmed in the served HTML on both the homepage and a policy page: the
copyright renders with no year, and a grep for `© 20xx` returns nothing.

